body: "## Static compatibility regression in PR #189\nExact reviewed head: `2d83808dd35ba96a19406deca49373976048d073`, compared with `75acc52aa905db1aee0c5f694f05d16b86e49c4b`.\n\nThe new forward-only importer is documented honestly: earlier successful items remain applied, per-item conflicts/uncertain outcomes are reported, and SDK read-to-write limitations are disclosed. This issue is **not** a request to weaken those safeguards or restore unsafe rollback.\n\nThe compatibility problem is that existing callers still explicitly request the former atomic contract:\n- CLI accepts `--atomic` on import/sync; its new help calls it a deprecated compatibility flag.\n- [`SyncOptions.atomic`](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-linear/blob/2d83808dd35ba96a19406deca49373976048d073/index.ts#L2691-L2695) and [`importLinearAtomic`](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-linear/blob/2d83808dd35ba96a19406deca49373976048d073/index.ts#L3370-L3395) remain callable.\n- The latter now invokes the forward-only coordinator and returns `recovered:false` plus a descriptive `linear-forward-…` transactionId, not a durable recovery journal.\n- [Coordinator](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-linear/blob/2d83808dd35ba96a19406deca49373976048d073/index.ts#L2774-L2925) applies native writes, retains prior successes after later failure, and reports `atomic:false`. Reporting the changed guarantee after writes is too late for an existing caller that explicitly requested the old mode.\n- Previously injectable batch commit seams are retained but inert; source/type compatibility is not behavioral compatibility.\n\n## Existing documented contract\n[Canonical pm-linear-nvt5](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-linear/blob/2d83808dd35ba96a19406deca49373976048d073/.agents/pm/tasks/pm-linear-nvt5.toon), delivered by [PR #36](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-linear/pull/36), explicitly records opt-in batch compensation and durable same-transaction recovery. This is the prior intended API contract, **not proof that old implementation supplied isolation against ordinary writers**; core #1576/#1620 limitations remain real.\n\nPR189's feature tracker reports a fleet directive toward forward-only operations. A separately accepted public migration decision overriding the explicit atomic-request contract was not verified by this review. The concrete request here is a safe compatibility boundary, independent of whether forward-only becomes the chosen default.\n\n## Expected vs actual / impact\nWhen a caller requests an unavailable stronger guarantee, fail before native mutations with a typed, actionable migration response, or provide that guarantee using supported shared SDK semantics. Do not silently interpret `--atomic` / the atomic-named SDK entrypoint as permission for partial forward-only application.\n\nExample existing workflow: import A and B with explicit atomic mode; B fails after A is applied. Under the new coordinator A intentionally stays applied. The caller must explicitly choose that tradeoff **before** writes, not discover it from the receipt.\n\nThe exported descriptive-fingerprint JSDoc also still says a reordered retry resumes a durable journal after adding a new non-executable-fingerprint disclaimer. Remove contradictory journal promises from the public helper documentation.\n\n## Evidence and limits\nStatic exact-source analysis and canonical record comparison. A bounded independent extraction of the exact coordinator passed 200 in-memory mocked scenarios /919 assertions, including retention of earlier writes, truthful unconfirmed/reconciled outcomes, dependency skips and omitted unchanged status. SDK/graph/read/write behavior was mocked; this is **not** a native atomic/rollback/concurrency reproduction. Current exact-head full CI was queued at review. No provider calls, native writes, lock/security probes or release actions were performed.\n\nAll-status issues/PRs and linked canonical records checked; no separate compatibility/migration issue found. PR189 remains implementation owner.\n\n## Acceptance\n1. Preserve a supported explicit atomic guarantee through reusable SDK primitives, **or** reject deprecated atomic requests before any mutation and require an explicit forward-only migration choice. Do not claim transaction isolation that the SDK cannot provide.\n2. Cover CLI aliases, `SyncOptions.atomic:true`, and `importLinearAtomic`; tests assert no mutation occurs when the requested guarantee is unavailable.\n3. Preserve the forward-only mode's truthful applied/skipped/unconfirmed receipts, prior successes, full history and foreign-write protections.\n4. Clearly distinguish descriptive receipt IDs from resumable transaction IDs; remove obsolete recovery/JSDoc promises and document source/API migration.\n5. Record the accepted capability tradeoff and validate packed consumers and supported SDK versions without weakening guards or merely changing old atomic assertions to accept partial writes.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (1)\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-10-29T23:31:53Z)\n>\n> Implemented in ff227e0 and 5de1ac1; current pushed head d96acb7c9fb029b8f4ea085b9c17e706ac0a33c6. Explicit CLI atomic requests, SyncOptions.atomic:true and importLinearAtomic refuse with atomic_unavailable before reads/writes unless forward-only migration is explicit. The executable schema-reviewed example now chooses forwardOnly:true; historical journal promises are removed. Evidence: node test/revert-import-boundaries.ts docs/import-boundaries-revert.json proves 14 independent behavior reversions fail and 8 restored tests pass. Packed artifact 7833dbef22e41e814eb87243f308dc122bb7c3b0083cddf3a88e159896095d10 passes all 10 npm/Bun consumer layouts on hosts 2026.10.24/2026.10.28: 830 commands, 310 SDK checks, 200 process-status cases, zero provider mutations. Native partial-save/observer/retry/history assertions remain intact. Full PR gates are queued, NOT verified: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-linear/actions/runs/40972907353 . No batch isolation or durable resume guarantee is claimed."
